  "metadata": {
    "title": "Simple Partnership: Capital and Profit Shares",
    "description": "Share profit or loss when all partners keep their money invested for the same length of time.",
    "teachingApproach": "Open with a two-person business where one partner puts in twice as much money and ask who should earn twice as much profit. Turn that fairness idea into the rule 'same time -> profit follows capital'. Then build through fractions of total capital, linked capital statements, reverse profit-to-capital reasoning and loss sharing. Every share question ends with a quick sum-back check.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 28,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 14, Partnership. Approved scope: Teach the central simple-partnership idea: when every partner stays for the same time, time cancels and profit or loss follows the capital ratio. Build from two partners to three, including fractional parts of total capital, linked statements such as A invests three times B, finding one share from total profit, finding total profit from one partner's share, and reversing a known profit ratio back to the capital ratio. Include loss-sharing as the same ratio rule. Keep the learner focused on 'same time -> compare money only', simplify ratios early, and verify that all shares add back to the stated total."
